notes:
Maintainer risk: MEDIUM — one real P0 correctness/security bug (body-buffer DOS on three transport paths) and several doc-drift bugs that propagate misinformation to downstream consumers; offset by strong defensive patterns already in place (credential-redaction canaries, SemVer-isolation wrappers, WS non-text-frame cap, streaming-with-cap in download_blob). Net: the existing patterns ARE the right ones; the work is propagating them consistently and keeping docs in sync.
Confidence: ~25% chance of missed bug. Least confident flags: JMAP-6r7c.1 (rests on the assumption that reqwest 0.12's resp.bytes() actually buffers the full body before returning — I inferred from API shape and the author's own comment at blob.rs:368-371, which explicitly says that pattern buffers, but did not read reqwest source); JMAP-6r7c.5 (the credential-leak risk via WsFrame::Unknown.raw is hypothetical for current JMAP types — it depends on a server emitting a malformed Response or a future extension type, neither of which has a concrete leak demonstrated today). Uncertainty driven by: (a) not running the tests, only reading them — a passing CI for this diff was assumed but not verified; (b) not tracing reqwest's internal buffering behavior to confirm the resp.bytes() allocation profile under chunked encoding without Content-Length; (c) async drop-mid-frame semantics of the SSE unfold loop not traced in detail — if a caller drops the BoxStream mid-iteration, raw_buf and buf are dropped cleanly by Rust ownership rules, but I did not verify there are no leaked tasks or async-runtime hazards; (d) WebSocket max_frame_size vs max_message_size interaction in tungstenite — set to the same value, which is correct but unusual and not load-tested. Would raise confidence if: ran cargo test --workspace --all-features green; ran cargo miri test on the SSE buf manipulation paths to confirm no UB in the char-boundary walk-back; loaded reqwest 0.12 source to confirm resp.bytes() implementation; ran a fuzz harness against parse_sse_block and parse_ws_frame with arbitrary byte inputs.
API contract risk: MEDIUM — the SemVer-isolation pattern (HttpError/WebSocketError/InvalidHeaderValueError wrappers) is in place and works, but is applied asymmetrically: serde_json::Error leaks via ClientError::Parse/Serialize (.26), reqwest::Client leaks via TransportConfig::build_client (.36), and jmap_cid_types::Sha256 is a workspace-internal re-export that locks downstream consumers to that crate's major version (.30). The trait shapes for AuthProvider (.20) and TransportConfig (.36) are too narrow to express the real-world auth schemes (OAuth refresh, SigV4) and TLS configurations (multi-cert chains, mTLS, PEM input) that downstream consumers will need; today every consumer needing more must roll their own. Concurrency contract on WsSession (.31) blocks the canonical one-send-task + one-receive-task topology that every real-world WebSocket consumer needs. Several defensive-code-forcing patterns exist: api_url-as-string (.39), template-vs-plain-URL untyped (.40), HttpError predicate dispatch (.34). Many are pre-1.0 acceptable; flagging now so the next major bump can land them in one pass.
API contract confidence: 20% chance of missed issue.

Least confident flags:
- JMAP-6r7c.20 (AuthProvider single-header lock): rests on the unverified assumption that an AWS SigV4 or OAuth-refresh consumer is a realistic near-term workspace user; if every actual downstream lives in the workspace and uses Bearer/Basic/None only, this is over-flagged.
- JMAP-6r7c.31 (WsSession concurrent send): I did not check whether downstream consumers (kith, stoa, the reference jmapchat-server) actually need concurrent send-while-receive — they might consistently use the single-task pattern that the current API supports fine.
- JMAP-6r7c.36 (TransportConfig leaks reqwest::Client): the right fix is acknowledged in error.rs:5-13 SemVer policy but not actually applied here. I am confident this is a real asymmetric leak; less confident on whether the workspace cares (reqwest may be intended as a permanent dep).

Uncertainty driven by:
(a) not surveying downstream consumers (kith, stoa, jmapchat-server) to verify which of these API gaps actually bite — many of my findings depend on hypothetical third-party consumers that may never exist outside the workspace, where the cookie-cutter discipline mitigates most contract drift;
(b) not running cargo test --workspace to confirm the diff is in a buildable state;
(c) not reading reqwest 0.12 source to verify the exact semantics of the leaky types I flagged (e.g., is reqwest::Client really a Send+Sync Arc-backed handle, or might it become non-Clone in a future minor version?);
(d) not investigating whether the workspace has already-filed major-version-bump epics that would naturally clean these up.

Would raise confidence if: (1) ran cargo doc --no-deps -p jmap-base-client --all-features and inspected the generated rustdoc for missing-info gaps that aren't visible from source-reading alone; (2) checked downstream consumers' Cargo.lock to see which jmap-base-client public types they actually call vs. just declare a dep on; (3) ran cargo public-api on a baseline to enumerate the actual SemVer surface this crate ships today.

Bikeshedding flag: four findings marked --add-label opinion (.41 unit-in-name, .42 pub-const-lock, .45 SseFrame.id conflation, .46 JmapRequest::new coupling) are pure forward-major-version flags or naming preferences with no engineering pressure on the current 0.x crate. Filed for completeness, but a /do-beads pass that closes them as wontfix is the right outcome unless the workspace is planning the next major bump.
Idiom rating: ACCEPTABLE — code is conventional Rust 2024-edition with a handful of mild non-idioms (extract_response mut-Option accumulator, compute_sha256_hex fold-with-mut, upload_blob positional args, DownloadBlobParams.expected_sha256 primitive obsession). No unsafe, no Box<dyn Error> in library API, no panic!/unreachable! outside provably-unreachable nibble bounds, no .unwrap() in production code, manual Debug impls correctly redact credentials. The two real type-design weaknesses (.48 expected_sha256 untyped, .50 upload_blob positional args) are forward-major-version flags, not present-tense bugs; both have working defensive validation today. The non-idiomatic patterns flagged are local — they do not propagate as the cookie-cutter shape because *-client siblings have not yet duplicated extract_response or compute_sha256_hex verbatim. Net: a serious Rust practitioner would nod, not wince.
Rust idiom confidence: ~30% chance of missed issue. Least confident flags: .47 (extract_response refactor — the cookie-cutter argument depends on whether jmap-mail-client and jmap-chat-client actually duplicate the pattern, which I did not verify; if they do not yet duplicate it, the propagation cost is lower and the priority drops); .50 (upload_blob params struct — depends on whether the workspace has a hard rule that any *-client method with >3 string params must be struct-shaped, which is implicit in DownloadBlobParams but never explicitly written down). Uncertainty driven by: (a) not running cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings to confirm the diff is clean under current lints — would catch any clippy::* findings I missed by reading; (b) not surveying the sibling *-client crates (jmap-mail-client, jmap-chat-client) to verify how many of the non-idioms here have already propagated; (c) not running cargo +nightly clippy with pedantic enabled to surface the long-tail of forward-looking lints. Would raise confidence if: (1) ran cargo clippy --workspace -- -D warnings and inspected the output; (2) ran cargo clippy -p jmap-base-client --all-features -- -W clippy::pedantic to see what pedantic flags; (3) inspected jmap-mail-client and jmap-chat-client for already-propagated copies of extract_response / compute_sha256_hex patterns. Bikeshedding flag: three findings labeled 'opinion' (.51 ClientConfig::validate repetition, .52 u64 cast pedantic, .54 decode_utf8_chunk doc comment) are pure aesthetics with no engineering pressure — a /do-beads pass should close them as wontfix. Real findings: 4 (.47, .48, .49, .50) — the remaining are flagged for completeness but acknowledged as low-priority forward-looking flags.
